Form: **have / has + past participle** (*have eaten, has gone, have finished*). Use it for:
- an action in the past with a **result now**: *I have lost my pen* (I still do not have it);
- **experience up to now**: *She has visited Douala twice*;
- actions with **just, already, yet, ever, never, since, for**: *We have just arrived. He has lived here for six years.*
With a **finished time** (yesterday, in 2019) use the simple past instead.
